# fixedbugs, defect d-lane-win-longname-1, 2026-11-27, lane win-paths
# (M-rebel-library step 212g).
#
# SYMPTOM: a module whose path the system will not open for its length was
# told as one that is not there. On Windows, a source of 180 letters read
# from a checkout folder of 43 characters, 260 in all, was told `there is no
# module` and *the directory lists it as* the same name, with `fix (guess):
# write use <the same line>`, exit 1; on this Mac a `use` whose file's name
# takes 256 bytes stopped the compilation at exit 2, *the system will not
# look at it*, naming neither the `use` nor the length nor the limit.
#
# FIX: a source's path is asked of the system's limit before its failure is
# read (`selfhost/cli/path_room.hero`'s `beyond`, the whole path and each
# name in it), and one past it is told on its `use` line, the path, its
# length and the limit, with no repair: no spelling of the line makes the
# folder shorter. The words are POSIX's, a name of 256 bytes past NAME_MAX's
# 255; Windows judges the whole path first, which names the checkout's own
# folder, so the case's words are judged off Windows and its mark on all
# three (`tests/harness/platform_skips.hero`). No file of that name is in the
# tree, nor can be on any of the three.
use amodulewhosefilesnametakestwohundredandfiftysixbytesonemorethanonenameinapathmaytakeonanyofthethreesystemssothesystemwillnotopenitanditsuseistoldsowiththelengthandthelimitandnorepairsincenospellingofthelinemakesitsfoldershorteroritsnameshorteranywhere  #~ unknown_module

function main()
    print(
        amodulewhosefilesnametakestwohundredandfiftysixbytesonemorethanonenameinapathmaytakeonanyofthethreesystemssothesystemwillnotopenitanditsuseistoldsowiththelengthandthelimitandnorepairsincenospellingofthelinemakesitsfoldershorteroritsnameshorteranywhere.f()
    )
